# Who gets pulled into a review, and why this file names a TEAM rather than people.
#
# Two constraints meet here. The first is `topology-clean`'s `operator residue`
# class, which forbids the operator's own GitHub handle anywhere in the tree —
# docs and config speak ROLES, so that a public repo is not tied to one person's
# fleet. The second is that CODEOWNERS accepts nothing but a literal @handle or
# @org/team. A team satisfies both: `Synapsium-Labs` is on that class's pass
# list, and the membership lives in the org where it can change without a commit.
#
# It is also simply the better shape. A file listing people goes stale the first
# time somebody joins or leaves, silently, in a way no suite can catch — the
# entry keeps resolving to an account that has stopped reading the repo.
#
# GitHub auto-requests this team on every pull request, and that is the whole job
# today: `require_code_owner_reviews` is deliberately OFF in `main`'s branch
# protection, so the one required approval may come from ANY collaborator with
# write access. Turning the flag on would restrict it to this team — a real
# change with a real victim (a maintainer outside the team finds their approval
# no longer counts), so make it on purpose, not as a side effect of this file.
#
# Ordering note for whoever adds the second rule: CODEOWNERS is LAST-MATCH-WINS,
# unlike .gitignore. A later, narrower line REPLACES this one for the paths it
# matches rather than adding to it, so an area that wants an extra owner must
# name every owner on its own line.

* @Synapsium-Labs/maintainers
